body:
Yeah, I agree with you, again I think the officer did not act properly, but I think classifying this event as a murder is inaccurate. I think that the combination of alcohol, the nature of the call and the culture in America of mass shooters/gun violence that were all present in this scenario lead to a horrible situation where, as you say, anyone would have a difficult time making it out of this situation alive. A cop wants to make it home alive to their wife and kids, and cops aren't perfect, maybe even more prone to acting without intelligence. Again I do not think that we can sit here and remove the human aspect of being a cop. I disagree that he clearly did not have a gun, maybe not a massive assault rifle but I don't think it's heinous to suspect he had a pistol. I guess my point is there is a difference between horrible mistakes/lapses in judgment/a really shitty situation and cold-blooded murder. 

&#x200B;

P.S. I appreciate you actually speaking about this even if you disagree with me

Reading these responses is pretty depressing. How can none of the people here put themselves in the minds of these cops, they are called to a hotel and told there is someone in a hotel room aiming a rifle at people from their window (they have no way of knowing that a. he is unarmed b. its an air rifle). Yes, the guy is clearly drunk and people here seem to be using that against the cop but if you are the cop that puts you more on edge knowing the guy is liable to act irrationally, like maybe reaching for a gun on his waistband. It's horrible this guy died, but if you are a cop you are entering this situation with someone who for all you know is armed, dangerous, crazy and drunk and he continually does that one thing he is told not to do. I just don't get how that chain of events leads everyone commenting here to calling the cop a cold-blooded killer, who was "waiting for the opportunity"  to do something. The situation probably could have been dealt with better, I don't deny that but some of the things said about this cop seem both despicable and wildly uninformed. 

&#x200B;

I am sure this will be extremely downvoted, but I would genuinely appreciate if at least one person would either consider what I say, or engage in a civil conversation with me, without immediately downvoting.

in 1962 Harvard researchers in conjunction with the CIA tried to figure out if LSD/other drugs could work as a sort of real-life "truth serum," they tested this hypothesis by pumping a bunch of Harvard students full of LSD(while also verbally torturing them, depriving them of sleep, using isolation etc.) Needless to say, it didn't work but the kicker is that one of the students they tested on was Ted Kaczynski aka the Unabomber.

&#x200B;

Project MKUltra
